`,e.jsx(n.h3,{id:"detour-query-and-rebuild-chain",children:"Detour query and rebuild chain"}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"1. Resolve endpoints and budgets."})," API documentation says ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"findNearestPoly"})," can leave ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"nearestRef = 0"})," and the output point unchanged when nothing matches; implementation still returns success after that search. Require nonzero endpoint references and record search extents, snapped positions, agent/build parameters and filter. Keep search-node budget, polygon-corridor capacity and straight-point capacity separate; ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"init"})," documents at most 65,535 nodes. [S3–S4]"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"2. Classify corridor results."})," ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"findPath"})," returns ordered polygon references. The documented undersized-buffer behavior is a prefix from the start; unreachable destinations return a best available corridor. Static implementation can combine ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"DT_SUCCESS"})," with ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"DT_PARTIAL_RESULT"}),", ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"DT_OUT_OF_NODES"})," and/or ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"DT_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL"}),". The success helper checks only its high-level bit. Preserve the entire status, count, capacities and final polygon. A capacity-truncated corridor need not have PARTIAL_RESULT when the search itself reached the destination. Growing the output buffer does not increase the search-node pool. [S5–S6]"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"3. Own sliced-query state."})," The documented sequence is initialize, update within iteration budgets until terminal, then finalize. The filter pointer must survive the query; source documentation warns that intervening non-sliced calls can corrupt its state. Static inspection shows update can report success after exhausting the frontier; finalization adds the partial-result detail and resets query state. ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"finalizeSlicedFindPathPartial"})," intentionally recovers a visited portion of an existing corridor and can lack PARTIAL_RESULT when such a polygon is found. Therefore compare the returned endpoint with the requested goal regardless of flags. Proposed adapter gate: isolate query ownership and invalidate/restart after relevant navigation changes; snapshot consistency, cancellation rollback and concurrent mutation safety were not established. [S3, S7]"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"4. Extract corners without upgrading reachability."})," ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"findStraightPath"})," clamps endpoints to the first/last supplied polygons. Its END flag and zero endpoint polygon reference identify that corridor's endpoint, not original-target arrival. An invalid intermediate portal can yield success plus partial output. At this pin, filling the straight-point capacity can set BUFFER_TOO_SMALL even when END is emitted: treat saturation conservatively and retry with headroom where needed. Keep off-mesh traversal execution as a separate game action. [S8]"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"5. Revalidate after obstacle edits."})," Documentation invalidates tile/polygon references on tile removal, while changing polygon flags/areas leaves references intact. Restoring the original reference is possible only under the documented original-parameters/reference conditions. Tile-cache rebuild implementation removes/replaces tiles without requesting the old reference; cached corridors therefore need freshness and traversal checks. [S9, S13]"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["Obstacle additions/removals enqueue work. The documented completion surface is repeated ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"update(..., &upToDate)"}),"; static inspection rebuilds at most one queued tile per call. At the exact commit above, it removes pending work and advances obstacle bookkeeping even after a rebuild failure; upToDate merely tests empty queues. This is a static source observation, not a reproduced engine bug. Preserve every update status: later successful drain cannot erase an earlier failure. Hardcoded capacities are eight touched tiles per obstacle and 64 request/update entries, not guaranteed useful coverage; bounded tile collection can truncate silently. Proposed gate: validate footprint/burst bounds, retain rebuild failures and requery affected routes after drain. [S10–S12]"]}),`
`,e.jsx(n.h3,{id:"evidence-and-availability-boundary",children:"Evidence and availability boundary"}),`
`,e.jsx(n.p,{children:"Record pin, navmesh generation, filter, input/snapped endpoints, capacities, all statuses, returned corridor/corners and obstacle-update history. Proposed acceptance requires the intended endpoint plus fresh game-level collision, locomotion, off-mesh-action and arrival evidence. A valid navmesh path never establishes collision-free game arrival by itself."}),`
`,e.jsx(n.p,{children:"Recast/Detour are available C++ libraries under the zlib license; DetourCrowd is a separate movement/avoidance module. Preserve applicable license notices and alteration attribution. Availability is not an installed GameMap adapter, MCP server or tested engine integration. Persistence, crash recovery and whole-game reset remain unverified. [S1–S2]"}),`
